Exp 1: 
    VTH0. Raising VTH0 from 0.30 V to 0.55 V shifts the transfer curve to the right, so IOFF falls 
    rom about 10⁻⁷ A/µm to about 3×10⁻¹⁰ A/µm, a drop of more than two orders of magnitude. ION falls much less,
    from about 1350 µA/µm to about 950 µA/µm at VGS = VDS = 1.1 V, since it depends only on the gate overdrive

Exp 2:
    TOXE. Increasing TOXE from 1.5 nm to 2.5 nm lowers the gate capacitance and weakens gate control, 
    which raises the effective threshold voltage and shifts the transfer curve to the right. 
    IOFF drops from about 2×10⁻⁸ A/µm to about 1×10⁻⁹ A/µm, more than an order of magnitude, and ION falls from about 1230 µA/µm 
    to about 1000 µA/µm at VGS = VDS = 1.1 V because of the smaller inversion charge

Exp 3:
    Increasing L from 55 nm to 100 nm lowers the drive current, since the longer channel has a lower lateral field 
    and higher channel resistance, so ION falls from about 1640 µA/µm to about 760 µA/µm at VGS = VDS = 1.1 V.
    IOFF drops from about 10⁻⁷ A/µm to about 10⁻⁹ A/µm because short-channel effects such as DIBL
    and the ID–VD curve of the 100 nm device is also much flatter in saturation, which shows reduced channel-length modulation.

Exp 4:
    Making XL less negative, from -35 nm to -15 nm, increases the physical gate length (L + XL goes from 30 nm to 50 nm) and 
    the effective channel length. This lowers the drive current, so ION falls from about 1320 µA/µm to about 920 µA/µm at
    VGS = VDS = 1.1 V, and IOFF falls from about 2×10⁻⁸ A/µm to about 2×10⁻⁹ A/µm because short-channel effects weaken in the 
    longer channel. The trend matches Exp 3, but the effect is smaller since XL changes the effective length by only 20 nm.

Exp 5:
    Increasing LINT from 2 nm to 8 nm increases the source/drain overlap under the gate on each side, 
    which shortens the effective channel (Leff = L + XL - 2·LINT, from about 31 nm to 19 nm). The drive current rises, with ION going
    from about 1025 µA/µm to about 1340 µA/µm at VGS = VDS = 1.1 V, and IOFF rises from about 3×10⁻⁹ A/µm to about 2×10⁻⁸ A/µm 
    because short-channel effects such as DIBL become stronger.

Exp 6:
    Increasing W from 0.5 µm to 2 µm scales the current almost in proportion to width, since a wider channel carries more carriers
    in parallel. ION rises from about 470 µA to about 1880 µA at VGS = VDS = 1.1 V, and IOFF rises from about 3×10⁻⁹ A to about
    1.4×10⁻⁸ A. The curves do not shift along the gate-voltage axis, so VTH is essentially unchanged, and the plotted current 
    is the absolute current for each width, not a per-µm value.

Exp 7:
    Increasing WINT from 0 nm to 40 nm reduces the effective width (Weff = W - 2·WINT, from 1 µm to about 0.92 µm),
    so ION at VGS = VDS = 1.1 V falls only slightly, from about 1160 µA/µm to about 1100 µA/µm, which is a small, 
    roughly proportional drop. The subthreshold curves overlap, so IOFF and VTH are essentially unchanged on the log plot

Exp 8:
    Increasing NDEP from 1.5×10¹⁸ to 4×10¹⁸ cm⁻³ raises VTH through the larger depletion charge and also lowers the mobility,
    so ION at VGS = VDS = 1.1 V falls modestly, from about 1220 µA/µm to about 1105 µA/µm. The effect on IOFF looks 
    opposite to the expected trend: the curves cross near VG = 0.2 V and IOFF at VG = 0 actually rises slightly, from 
    about 5×10⁻⁹ A/µm to about 1.1×10⁻⁸ A/µm. This is because heavier doping increases the depletion capacitance 
    and because BSIM4 also uses NDEP in its short-channel and body-effect terms

Exp 9:
    The curves differ by only about 1% at VG = 0 and by well under 0.1% at VGS = VDS = 1.1 V (about 1150 µA/µm), so 
    IOFF, ION and VTH are effectively unchanged. Physically, heavier source/drain doping should lower the series resistance
    and raise ION, but this is not seen because BSIM4 treats NSD separately from RDSW: NSD only enters junction built-in potential 
    used in the S/D diode and short-channel terms

Exp 10:
    Increasing XJ from 10 nm to 30 nm has very little effect, with the transfer curves overlapping and IOFF unchanged 
    and ION rising only slightly. Physically a deeper junction should worsen short-channel effects such as DIBL and raise IOFF,
    but in BSIM4 XJ enters only through the short-channel correction terms, and the DIBL strength is set mainly by ETA0 and DSUB, 
    so the compact model shows a much weaker dependence than a TCAD device would.

Exp 11:
    Lowering NGATE from 10²¹ to 10¹⁹ cm⁻³ makes poly-gate depletion significant at high gate bias, which adds a series 
    depletion capacitance and reduces the inversion charge, so the transfer curve bends over at high VG and ION falls
    The subthreshold region and IOFF are unchanged because poly depletion only matters at high gate voltage. 
    On the ID–VD plot at VGS = 1.1 V, the low-NGATE curve lies well below the others over the whole sweep, with a smaller current
    in the linear region and a lower saturation level, because the effective gate overdrive is reduced

Exp 12:
    Increasing U0 from 300 to 700 cm²/V·s raises the current at all gate voltages, with IOFF rising from about 5×10⁻⁹ A/µm to
    about 9×10⁻⁹ A/µm because drift current scales with mobility, and the curves keep the same slope and position on the gate-voltage
    axis, so VTH is unchanged. ION at VGS = VDS = 1.1 V rises much less than the 3 times increase in U0, because the short 65 nm ch
    is limited by velocity saturation. On the ID–VD plot the low-U0 curve is lower in the linear region where as the three curves
    move closer together in saturation, where the current is set by VSAT.

Exp 13:
    Increasing VSAT from 0.8×10⁷ to 2×10⁷ cm/s raises the on-state current strongly, with ION at VGS = VDS = 1.1 V going from about
    890 µA/µm to about 1460 µA/µm, since the 65 nm channel is velocity-saturated and the saturation current scales with VSAT.
    The subthreshold region and IOFF (about 7×10⁻⁹ A/µm) are unchanged because the current there is diffusion-limited,
    so VTH does not move. On the ID–VD plot the curves with the high-VSAT device saturating at a higher voltage and a higher 
    current level, and the low-VSAT device saturating early and flattening. This is the same behaviour seen in S4, where 
    removing velocity saturation gave the largest increase in ION.

Exp 14:
    Increasing RDSW from 50 to 400 Ω·µm adds series resistance at the source and drain, so the effective gate and drain voltages
    across the intrinsic channel are reduced and ION at VGS = VDS = 1.1 V falls strongly, from about 1560 µA/µm to about 800 µA/µm.
    The subthreshold curves and IOFF overlap completely because the current there is too small to drop any voltage across resistance,
    and VTH is therefore unchanged. On the ID–VD plot the slope in the linear region is much lower for large RDSW, since the series
    resistance dominates the total on-resistance, and the saturation level is also reduced.

    